\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Irreducible modules on a translation orbit}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Use the grid group and field of \ref{fdip-016E}. Its irreducible
representations are obtained by taking one label #{r} per orbit and
the \vocabk{induced module}{fdip-016C} of #{F(r)} for each irreducible
#{k}-representation #{F} of #{G_r}. Two entries are equivalent
exactly when the representative and the #{G_r}-type agree. If
#{|G|} is invertible in #{k}, a finite-dimensional representation #{V}
of #{G_\Lambda} is a direct sum of these modules. The multiplicity
at #{r,F} equals that of #{F} in #{V_r} as a #{G_r}-module.}

\proof{
\p{In #{k[G_\Lambda]\otimes_{k[G_{\Lambda,r}]}F(r)}, the left coset
representatives #{b} give copies #{b\otimes F}, since the group
algebra is the direct sum of the free right subgroup-algebra modules
#{b\,k[G_{\Lambda,r}]}. On each such copy translation by #{x}
acts by #{\alpha_r(b^{-1}x)=\alpha_{br}(x)}. A nonzero submodule
is preserved by the projections of \ref{fdip-016E}, hence contains
a nonzero vector in one copy. Transport it to the copy at #{r}.
Irreducibility of #{F} supplies that whole copy, then transport
supplies every other copy. Conversely an irreducible #{V} has only one
nonzero orbit summand by \ref{fdip-016E}. A proper nonzero
#{G_r}-submodule of #{V_r} would transport to a proper nonzero
submodule across that orbit, by the same coset construction.
An intertwining isomorphism preserves every translation block.
The orbit and the representation on its chosen block therefore
recover the original data.}

\p{For the direct sum assertion, average a linear projection onto
any #{G_r}-invariant subspace of #{V_r} over its conjugates by
#{G_r}. Its order divides #{|G|} and is invertible in #{k}.
The average fixes the subspace pointwise, has image in it and
commutes with #{G_r}. Its kernel is an invariant complement.
Induction on dimension splits #{V_r} into irreducibles, and
tensoring distributes over their direct sum. An equivariant map
between irreducibles is zero or an isomorphism by its kernel and
image. Hence #{\operatorname{End}_{G_r}(F)} is a division algebra,
and the dimension of #{\operatorname{Hom}_{G_r}(F,V_r)} as a
right space over it, acting by precomposition, counts the copies.
This argument also underlies the multiplicities in \ref{fdip-013S}.}

\p{On the fields of \ref{fdip-016D},
every block has dimension #{\dim_k F} for the supplied fiber #{F}.
One orbit thus contributes #{|Gr|\dim_k F} to the dimension. Summing
over orbits gives #{|\Lambda|\dim_k F}.}
}

\p{Serre treats the complex representations of a finite semidirect
product with abelian normal subgroup in
\citet{§8.2, Proposition 25, pp. 62-63}{serre1977linear}.
The character projections above use only the stated roots and
invertibility in #{k}. Mathlib's
\leanref{MonoidAlgebra.Submodule.exists_isCompl} gives the invariant
complement for a group algebra in the nondividing characteristic.}
